{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman who played a key role in the history of the County of Tripoli during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He succeeded his father Raymond II in 1187 and became the last independent ruler of the county before its eventual absorption into the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Born around 1150 Raymond III was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Tripoli who was in turn the daughter of Raymond II of Antioch. His mother’s influence and lineage gave him strong ties to the ruling elite of the Crusader states.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Inheritance**\nRaymond III’s early years were marked by the political turmoil in the Crusader states. His father Raymond II died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin where Saladin’s forces inflicted a devastating defeat on the Christian army. Despite the broader collapse of Crusader defenses Raymond III managed to hold onto Tripoli. His mother Hodierna served as regent until his majority was reached around 1190. During this period Raymond III faced immediate threats from Saladin who aimed to consolidate his control over the remaining Crusader territories. Raymond’s inheritance included not only Tripoli but also extensive lands in Antioch thanks to his mother’s lineage.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was defined by his efforts to maintain independence and security for Tripoli. His primary challenge was balancing relations with both the Ayyubid dynasty led by Saladin and the Kingdom of Jerusalem which was struggling under the rule of Isaac Komnenos and later Conrad of Montferrat. In 1189 he married Eschiva of Bures a noblewoman from the Kingdom of Jerusalem to strengthen his political alliances. The marriage produced a son named Bohemond who would later become Raymond’s heir.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2*\n**Military and Political Struggles**\nRaymond III’s military prowess was tested early in his reign when he led defenses against Saladin’s armies. In 1188 he engaged Saladin’s forces at the Battle of Isso near modern-day Tartus where he suffered a defeat. This battle underscored the growing power of the Ayyubid dynasty and the vulnerability of the Crusader states. Despite this setback Raymond III continued to negotiate with Saladin seeking temporary truces to secure his borders. His diplomatic skills were crucial in delaying Saladin’s advances and buying time for Tripoli’s survival.\n\nInternally Raymond III faced opposition from some of his barons who favored closer ties with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He also had to manage the complex relationships within the Crusader states particularly with Antioch and Jerusalem. By the early 1200s Raymond III’s health began to decline which weakened his political influence. His son Bohemond was groomed to take over the countship but Raymond III’s declining health and the instability in the region made governing increasingly difficult.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years and Legacy**\nRaymond III’s later years were marked by efforts to secure the future of Tripoli. He continued negotiations with Saladin’s successors particularly al-Adil I who succeeded Saladin in 1196. These negotiations often involved tribute payments which were a common practice among the Crusader states to avoid outright conflict. Raymond III’s diplomatic approach helped maintain a fragile peace allowing Tripoli to remain independent despite the broader decline of Crusader territories.\n\nIn 1216 Raymond III died leaving the County of Tripoli to his son Bohemond. Bohemond’s reign however proved to be short-lived as he died in 1230. Without a direct heir the county of Tripoli eventually passed to Bohemond’s sister Maria who married Odo of Montbéliard. Despite Raymond III’s efforts to preserve Tripoli’s independence the county’s fate was closely tied to the broader struggles of the Crusader states. His reign is often remembered as a period of resilience and diplomacy in the face of overwhelming odds. Raymond III’s legacy lies in his ability to keep Tripoli autonomous in a time when other Crusader states were falling to Muslim forces.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035124807618558407}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.018119992688298225}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.49807846546173096}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.11175623536109924}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.2499924898147583}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.00010275312524754554}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.6992132067680359}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.11881808191537857}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.9773544669151306}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -1.099503517150879}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.033750541508197784}, {"token": " key", "logprob": -2.8323376178741455}, {"token": " role", "logprob": -0.003928089048713446}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.7593773007392883}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.02604844607412815}, {"token": " history", "logprob": -0.30133137106895447}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00014411364099942148}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0153661435469985}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.6782187223434448}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.5152843591058627e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.00954103097319603}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.3234706223011017}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00655180960893631}, {"token": " late", "logprob": -1.2705423831939697}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0233613271266222}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.245071224635467e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0183948315680027}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -1.1205610462639015e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.14955788850784302}, {"token":